          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Major Works and Theories</strong><br>\nAmong Freud's influential works are \"The Interpretation of Dreams,\" \"The Ego and the Id,\" and \"Civilization and Its Discontents.\" His theories on the Oedipus complex, libido, and the structure of the psyche significantly impacted the understanding of human psychology.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Controversies and Criticism</strong><br>\nFreud's theories often stirred controversy, drawing criticism for their emphasis on sexuality and for being unscientific. His ideas sparked debates that have persisted long after his death, influencing various fields, including literature, art, and popular culture.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life and Death</strong><br>\nFreud married Martha Bernays in 1886, with whom he had six children. Fleeing Nazi persecution, Freud died in exile in the United Kingdom on September 23, 1939, after a battle with mouth cancer.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
